 ## Overview
-Classical Ray Tracing Integration is a volume rendering technique that computes pixel color by combining background transmission (attenuated by absorption) and uniform in-scattering [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering volumetric objects with defined boundaries or integrating geometric visibility directly into volume pipelines, offering a closed-form solution for homogeneous volumes or a deferred shading heuristic for complex scalar fields [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike standard direct volume ray casting which samples uniformly along the entire ray, this method explicitly calculates entry and exit points to limit sampling to the volume interior and applies Beer's law for transmission [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from [Alpha Compositing Ray Casting](922-alpha-compositing-ray-casting.md) by its handling of homogeneous spheres via analytical integration, and from [Single Scattering Volume Rendering with Ray Marching](1532-single-scattering-volume-rendering-with-ray-marching.md) by its assumption of uniform scattering color [S1, unofficial].
+Classical Ray Tracing Integration is a volume rendering technique that computes pixel color by combining background transmission (attenuated by absorption) and uniform in-scattering [S2, S5, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering volumetric objects with defined boundaries or integrating geometric visibility directly into volume pipelines [S1, S4, unofficial]. Unlike standard direct volume ray casting which samples uniformly along the entire ray, this method explicitly calculates entry and exit points to limit sampling to the volume interior and applies Beer's law for transmission [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is distinguished from [Alpha Compositing Ray Casting](922-alpha-compositing-ray-casting.md) by its handling of homogeneous spheres via analytical integration, and from [Single Scattering Volume Rendering with Ray Marching](1532-single-scattering-volume-rendering-with-ray-marching.md) by its assumption of uniform scattering color [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
