 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Redundant Distance Calculation**: Computing the Euclidean distance between entry and exit points ($\|\bar{p}_1 - \bar{p}_2\|$) is correct but computationally redundant if $t_0$ and $t_1$ are known; use $t_1 - t_0$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Uniform Scattering Assumption**: The analytical mode assumes uniform scattering color regardless of position or direction; it does not account for directional lighting or varying scattering properties [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Heuristic Gradient**: In `SINGLE_SHADE_HEURISTIC`, gradients are not accumulated; only the highest density point is stored for deferred shading [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Shadow Ray Cost**: Computing shadow transmittance from the highest density point adds significant cost for the heuristic mode [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Redundant Distance Calculation**: Computing the Euclidean distance between entry and exit points ($\|\bar{p}_1 - \bar{p}_2\|$) is correct but computationally redundant if $t_0$ and $t_1$ are known; use $t_1 - t_0$ [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Uniform Scattering Assumption**: The analytical mode assumes uniform scattering color regardless of position or direction; it does not account for directional lighting or varying scattering properties [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Heuristic Gradient**: In `SINGLE_SHADE_HEURISTIC`, gradients are not accumulated; only the highest density point is stored for deferred shading [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Shadow Ray Cost**: Computing shadow transmittance from the highest density point adds significant cost for the heuristic mode [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of samples along the ray [G]. For analytical spheres, it is $O(1)$ per pixel [S1, unofficial]. For marching, it depends on volume density and step size [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of samples along the ray [G]. For analytical spheres, it is $O(1)$ per pixel [S1, S2, unofficial]. For marching, it depends on volume density and step size [S2, S3, unofficial].
 *   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ for analytical mode. $O(V)$ for volume data storage in marching mode [G].
-*   **Performance**: Analytical integration is extremely fast and exact for homogeneous spheres [S1, unofficial]. Ray marching is slower but handles complex geometries [S2, unofficial]. Early ray termination optimizes performance in dense regions [S2, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Exact solution for homogeneous volumes; integrates geometric visibility; supports shading heuristics for complex data [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: Analytical mode is limited to simple shapes (spheres); uniform scattering assumption is unrealistic for many volumes; heuristic shading may produce artifacts if the highest density point is not representative [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: Analytical integration is extremely fast and exact for homogeneous spheres [S1, S2, unofficial]. Ray marching handles complex scalar fields and supports deferred shading heuristics [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Exact solution for homogeneous volumes; integrates geometric visibility; supports shading heuristics for complex data [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: Analytical mode is limited to simple shapes; uniform scattering assumption is unrealistic for many volumes; heuristic shading may produce artifacts if the highest density point is not representative [S1, S2, unofficial].
 *   **When to use**: Ideal for rendering simple volumetric objects (e.g., clouds, smoke balls) or when integrating geometric surfaces with volume data [S1, S3, unofficial]. Avoid for highly anisotropic or spatially varying scattering volumes [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from early volume rendering techniques that combined ray tracing with volume data [S1, unofficial]. It was formalized in the context of scientific visualization shading, where analytical integration for homogeneous spheres provided a baseline for more complex methods [S1, unofficial]. The integration of classical ray tracing into volume rendering pipelines was proposed to handle visibility and geometric merging, such as superimposing radiation treatment planning isodose surfaces over CT data [S3, unofficial]. The heuristic shading approach was developed to balance performance and visual quality in scientific visualization [S2, unofficial]. Recent applications include integrating ray tracing for interreflections in 3D Gaussian Splatting [S5, unofficial].
